One principle, several formulations
The small- and finite-deformation expressions can now be viewed together.
For small strain,
\[
\boxed{
\int_{\partial\Omega}
\mathbf t\cdot\delta\mathbf u\,dA
=
\int_\Omega
\boldsymbol{\sigma}:
\delta\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}
\,dV.
}
\]
For finite deformation in the current configuration,
\[
\boxed{
\int_{\partial\Omega_t}
\mathbf t\cdot\mathbf v\,da
=
\int_{\Omega_t}
\boldsymbol{\sigma}:\mathbf d\,dv.
}
\]
And in the reference configuration,
\[
\boxed{
\int_{\Omega_0}
\mathbf P:\dot{\mathbf F}\,dV
=
\int_{\Omega_0}
\mathbf S:\dot{\mathbf E}\,dV.
}
\]
The mathematical quantities change, but the energetic principle connecting external action to internal deformation remains.

Graduation is not the end of civil engineering education
Even an excellent university education cannot provide everything an engineer will need over a 30- or 40-year career.
Codes change. Materials and construction methods change. Computational tools change. Expectations concerning safety, sustainability, ethics, and public responsibility change. Technologies that did not exist when an engineer graduated may later become central to practice.
Civil engineering education therefore needs a longer time scale:
\[
\boxed{
\text{University Education}
\rightarrow
\text{Professional Formation}
\rightarrow
\text{Practice}
\rightarrow
\text{CPD}
\rightarrow
\text{Professional Leadership}
}
\]
Graduation is not the end of engineering education. It is closer to the beginning of professional formation.
University education and continuing professional development should therefore answer different parts of the same question.
Universities should emphasize durable knowledge and ways of reasoning. Practice should connect that knowledge to real systems, constraints, risks, and responsibilities. CPD should allow engineers to update and extend their professional map as technology and society change.

Offshore wind forces traditionally separate disciplines to work together
There is another important difference from much of conventional land-based construction.
Civil-engineering education is usually divided into recognizable subjects: structural engineering, geotechnical engineering, hydraulics, construction, transportation, and so on. That division is useful for learning the fundamentals.
Offshore wind is less willing to respect those boundaries.
A support-structure problem can simultaneously involve:
\[
\boxed{
\text{soil}
+\text{structure}
+\text{waves}
+\text{dynamics}
+\text{construction}
+\text{operations}
}
\]
For example, changing a foundation stiffness changes structural natural frequencies. Those frequencies affect dynamic response and fatigue. The installation method constrains geometry and fabrication. Hydrodynamic loading depends on the submerged structure. Maintenance strategy depends on accessibility and vessel operation.
The disciplines remain distinct, but the engineering decisions are coupled.
This is one reason offshore wind requires a more integrative form of civil engineering than many engineers encounter in conventional projects.

Hooke’s law emerges from this decomposition
Substituting
\[
\varepsilon_{ij}^{\mathrm{vol}}
=\frac{1}{3}\varepsilon_{kk}\delta_{ij}
\]
and
\[
\varepsilon_{ij}^{\mathrm{dev}}
=\varepsilon_{ij}-\frac{1}{3}\varepsilon_{kk}\delta_{ij}
\]
into the constitutive relation gives
\[
\sigma_{ij}
=K\varepsilon_{kk}\delta_{ij}
+2G\left(
\varepsilon_{ij}-\frac{1}{3}\varepsilon_{kk}\delta_{ij}
\right).
\]
Therefore,
\[
\sigma_{ij}
=2G\varepsilon_{ij}
+\left(K-\frac{2}{3}G\right)\varepsilon_{kk}\delta_{ij}.
\]
Define the Lamé constants
\[
\boxed{
\lambda=K-\frac{2}{3}G,
\qquad
\mu=G.
}
\]
Then
\[
\boxed{
\sigma_{ij}
=
\lambda\varepsilon_{kk}\delta_{ij}
+2\mu\varepsilon_{ij}.
}
\]
This is the tensor form of Hooke’s law for an isotropic linear elastic material.
The important point is the direction of the reasoning. We did not begin with \(\lambda\) and \(\mu\). We began with two physical deformation mechanisms and obtained the Lamé form from them.

Plane strain: constrain deformation
For two-dimensional work we use the in-plane ordering
\[
\boxed{11,\;22,\;12.}
\]
Plane strain imposes the kinematic constraints
\[
\boxed{
\varepsilon_{33}=\gamma_{23}=\gamma_{13}=0.
}
\]
But generally
\[
\sigma_{33}\neq0.
\]
The material develops whatever out-of-plane stress is required to prevent out-of-plane strain.
The in-plane constitutive relation becomes
\[
\boxed{
\begin{bmatrix}
\sigma_{11}\\
\sigma_{22}\\
\sigma_{12}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\frac{E}{(1+\nu)(1-2\nu)}
\begin{bmatrix}
1-\nu & \nu & 0\\
\nu & 1-\nu & 0\\
0 & 0 & \dfrac{1-2\nu}{2}
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\varepsilon_{11}\\
\varepsilon_{22}\\
\gamma_{12}
\end{bmatrix}.
}
\]
Thus
\[
\boxed{
\mathbf D_{\mathrm{plane\ strain}}
=
\frac{E}{(1+\nu)(1-2\nu)}
\begin{bmatrix}
1-\nu & \nu & 0\\
\nu & 1-\nu & 0\\
0 & 0 & \dfrac{1-2\nu}{2}
\end{bmatrix}.
}
\]
The out-of-plane stress is
\[
\boxed{
\sigma_{33}
=
\frac{E\nu}{(1+\nu)(1-2\nu)}
(\varepsilon_{11}+\varepsilon_{22}).
}
\]
This is the physical meaning of plane strain: the deformation is constrained, and stress appears to enforce that constraint.

Plane stress: constrain stress
Plane stress imposes instead
\[
\boxed{
\sigma_{33}=\sigma_{23}=\sigma_{13}=0.
}
\]
But generally
\[
\varepsilon_{33}\neq0.
\]
From the three-dimensional constitutive law, imposing \(\sigma_{33}=0\) gives
\[
\boxed{
\varepsilon_{33}
=-\frac{\nu}{1-\nu}
(\varepsilon_{11}+\varepsilon_{22}).
}
\]
The in-plane law is therefore
\[
\boxed{
\begin{bmatrix}
\sigma_{11}\\
\sigma_{22}\\
\sigma_{12}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\frac{E}{1-\nu^2}
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & \nu & 0\\
\nu & 1 & 0\\
0 & 0 & \dfrac{1-\nu}{2}
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\varepsilon_{11}\\
\varepsilon_{22}\\
\gamma_{12}
\end{bmatrix}.
}
\]
Thus
\[
\boxed{
\mathbf D_{\mathrm{plane\ stress}}
=
\frac{E}{1-\nu^2}
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & \nu & 0\\
\nu & 1 & 0\\
0 & 0 & \dfrac{1-\nu}{2}
\end{bmatrix}.
}
\]
Here is an important computational point: plane stress is not obtained by simply deleting the third row and column of the three-dimensional stiffness matrix.
The out-of-plane strain \(\varepsilon_{33}\) is unknown. It must first adjust so that \(\sigma_{33}=0\). Only after eliminating that unknown do we obtain the plane-stress matrix. In matrix language, the operation is a Schur complement, or constitutive static condensation.
That is why the plane-stress and plane-strain matrices look different even though both describe the same three-dimensional material.

My starting point: template.tex
Every new manuscript begins from a reusable template.
The purpose of template.tex is not to contain the scientific content of a particular paper. Its role is to establish the environment in which that content will be written.
A typical starting structure defines the locations of common resources, loads my shared packages and commands, establishes the local figure directory, and connects the manuscript to the master bibliography.
Conceptually, the template does something like this:
% common paths
\newcommand{\rootpath}{...}
\newcommand{\commonpath}{...}

% shared infrastructure
\input{\commonpath/zipackage.tex}
\input{\commonpath/zicommand.tex}

% local figures
\graphicspath{{./}}

% manuscript begins here
\begin{document}

...

\bibliography{.../reference}

\end{document}
The exact path definitions can change with the computer or folder structure, but the principle remains the same.
A new paper should not require me to reconstruct my entire LaTeX environment.
I start with the infrastructure already in place and concentrate on the research.
This also makes the beginning of a new project much less arbitrary. I already know where equations, figures, references, commands, and shared settings belong.

English | 한글
Tables look simple until engineering data are involved.
A real engineering table may need to show:
At that point, a table is no longer just a grid.
It is a compact representation of the structure of the experiment.
That is why LaTeX tables are worth learning properly.
The basic tabular environment is already powerful, but the most useful engineering tables usually depend on a small number of additional ideas:
\[
\boxed{
\text{column structure}
+
\text{grouped headings}
+
\text{row grouping}
+
\text{units}
+
\text{numerical alignment}
}
\]
The important question is therefore not:
A better question is:

What my existing template is doing
My working LaTeX template already contains several fairly sophisticated examples.
One table presents a concrete mix proportion with a two-level header. The first four columns contain individual variables, while four later columns are grouped under the heading weight. This is done with \multicolumn. Several heading cells extend vertically across two rows using \multirow, and \cline{5-8} draws only the horizontal rule beneath the grouped heading.
The core pattern is:
\begin{table}[htb]
\centering
\caption{The mix proportion of the concretes.}
\begin{small}
\begin{tabular}{c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c}
\hlinewd{1.5pt}
\multirow{2}{*}{...} &
\multirow{2}{*}{...} &
\multirow{2}{*}{...} &
\multirow{2}{*}{...} &
\multicolumn{4}{|c|}{weight [kgf]} &
\multirow{2}{*}{SP} &
\multirow{2}{*}{AE} \\
\cline{5-8}
& & & & w & c & s & g & & \\
\hline
...
\hlinewd{1.5pt}
\end{tabular}
\end{small}
\label{st:tab.mix}
\end{table}
This is an important pattern because the physical organization of the data is directly visible in the LaTeX source.
The table is saying:
mix properties | constituent weights | admixtures
rather than simply placing ten unrelated columns next to one another.
A second table in the template goes further. It groups several rows under labels such as apparent modulus, stiffness, and maximum load using \multirow, while \cline{2-7} separates the entries inside each group. Nested tabular environments are used to break long labels such as partially removed over two lines.
A third example groups three biaxial-test columns and three uniaxial-test columns under separate headings using \multicolumn.
These are exactly the kinds of structures that appear repeatedly in experimental mechanics and structural engineering.

makecell: cleaner multiline headings
My older tables often use nested tables:
\begin{tabular}{c}
partially \\
removed
\end{tabular}
This works, but it is verbose.
The makecell package gives a simpler alternative:
\makecell{partially \\ removed}
Likewise,
\makecell{apparent modulus\\(\si{\mega\pascal})}
is easier to read than inserting a miniature tabular environment inside a cell.
For tables with many multiline headings, makecell noticeably improves source readability.

threeparttable: notes belong to the table
Engineering tables frequently need notes such as:
* Mean value from three specimens.
** Specimen excluded because of instrumentation failure.
Putting such explanations into the main caption quickly becomes awkward.
The threeparttable package provides a clean structure:
\begin{threeparttable}
\caption{Test results.}

\begin{tabular}{...}
...
\end{tabular}

\begin{tablenotes}
\item[a] Mean of three tests.
\item[b] Sensor failure.
\end{tablenotes}
\end{threeparttable}
This keeps the caption, table, and notes as one semantic unit.

Packages I would use only when necessary
More packages are not automatically better.
A complicated preamble eventually becomes another system that has to be maintained.
For example, colored cells can be useful in presentations or design summaries, and xcolor together with table-color support can do this well. But color should communicate information, not decorate a numerical table.
Landscape tables can be handled with packages such as pdflscape, but a table that requires landscape orientation may first deserve a structural rethink.
Likewise, scaling an entire table with
\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{...}
is convenient, but it should not be the first response to an oversized table. Scaling can make the text unreadably small.
A better sequence is usually:
\[
\boxed{
\text{simplify}
\rightarrow
\text{group}
\rightarrow
\text{manage widths}
\rightarrow
\text{split if necessary}
\rightarrow
\text{scale only as a last resort}
}
\]

Why resonance changes in water
For a dry structure, a simplified modal resonance estimate is
\[
\omega_n
\sim
\sqrt{
\frac{K}{M}
}.
\]
For a floating structure, the analogous coupled interpretation is
\[
\omega_n
\sim
\sqrt{
\frac{
K+ C^{\mathrm{hydro}}
}{
M+A^{\mathrm{hydro}}
}
}.
\]
This is schematic rather than an exact multidimensional eigenvalue expression, but it reveals the physics immediately.
The surrounding water can:

increase effective inertia through added mass,
change restoring stiffness through hydrostatics,
and add damping through wave radiation.

The resonance of the floating system is therefore a property of the coupled fluid–structure system, not the dry structure alone.

The bridge problem is fundamentally a compatibility problem
For a free member under sustained bending moment,
\[
M=\text{constant}
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
\kappa(t)\uparrow.
\]
A real bridge is constrained. Prestressing steel does not creep like concrete. Reinforcement restrains deformation. Adjacent segments have different ages. Continuity restrains rotations. Bearings and piers impose compatibility. Shrinkage creates deformation without directly applying stress.
Thus,
\[
\boxed{
\text{creep}
\rightarrow
\text{deformation}
\rightarrow
\text{compatibility}
\rightarrow
\text{stress redistribution}
\rightarrow
\text{more creep}.
}
\]
This is why long-term box-girder deflection cannot generally be obtained by multiplying the initial elastic deflection by \(1+\phi\).

교량의 장기거동은 결국 적합조건의 문제다
일정한 휨모멘트를 받는 자유로운 콘크리트 부재라면
\[
M=\text{constant}
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
\kappa(t)\uparrow
\]
라고 생각할 수 있다.
하지만 실제 교량은 자유롭지 않다.
프리스트레싱 강재는 콘크리트와 같은 방식으로 크리프하지 않는다. 철근은 콘크리트 변형을 구속한다. 서로 다른 시기에 타설한 세그먼트는 재령도 다르다. 연속화된 구조는 회전을 구속한다. 받침과 교각도 적합조건을 만든다. 수축은 외력이 없어도 변형을 발생시킨다.
결국 과정은
\[
\boxed{
\text{크리프}
\rightarrow
\text{변형}
\rightarrow
\text{적합조건}
\rightarrow
\text{응력재분배}
\rightarrow
\text{추가 크리프}
}
\]
가 된다.
그래서 박스거더의 장기처짐을 단순히 초기 탄성처짐에 \(1+\phi\)를 곱해서 구할 수 없는 경우가 많다.

왜 물속에서는 공진주파수가 달라지는가?
건조 구조물의 단순화된 모드 공진주파수는 대략
\[
\omega_n
\sim
\sqrt{
\frac{K}{M}
}
\]
으로 생각할 수 있다.
부유식 구조물에서는 이에 대응하는 연성계의 물리적 해석이
\[
\omega_n
\sim
\sqrt{
\frac{
K+ C^{\mathrm{hydro}}
}{
M+A^{\mathrm{hydro}}
}
}
\]
가 된다.
물론 이것은 다차원 고유치문제를 정확하게 표현한 식이라기보다 물리를 보여주기 위한 개략식이다. 하지만 무엇이 달라지는지는 바로 보인다.
주변 물은

부가질량으로 유효관성을 증가시키고,
정수력으로 복원강성을 바꾸며,
파의 방사로 감쇠를 추가한다.

따라서 부유체의 공진은 건조 구조물만의 특성이 아니라 유체와 구조물이 결합된 시스템 전체의 특성이다.

